1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,423
Every year, freelancers and occasional sellers have to declare the money they

2
00:00:05,423 --> 00:00:07,042
receive through PayPal.

3
00:00:07,042 --> 00:00:12,606
This tool reads that history and explains, in plain language, what belongs in a

4
00:00:12,606 --> 00:00:13,944
Spanish tax return.

5
00:00:13,944 --> 00:00:16,103
PayPal statements are messy.

6
00:00:16,103 --> 00:00:22,580
They mix occasional sales, recurring income, refunds, currency conversions and fees.

7
00:00:22,580 --> 00:00:28,748
Classifying every movement by hand is slow, and one mistake can cost you time or

8
00:00:28,748 --> 00:00:29,211
money.

9
00:00:29,211 --> 00:00:35,380
This is exactly the kind of repetitive work that a small AI assistant can do for

10
00:00:35,380 --> 00:00:35,688
you.

11
00:00:35,688 --> 00:00:40,915
The tool uses the PayPal Transaction Search API to import your movements.

12
00:00:40,915 --> 00:00:46,787
An AI classifier assigns each one a fiscal category, and the European Central Bank

13
00:00:46,787 --> 00:00:49,222
rates convert everything to euros.

14
00:00:49,222 --> 00:00:55,022
The output is a detailed CSV plus a plain-language summary that tells you what to

15
00:00:55,022 --> 00:00:57,600
declare and what to review manually.

16
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:00,651
Here is the full pipeline in mock mode.

17
00:01:00,651 --> 00:01:07,144
Twenty-four sample transactions go in: occasional sales, recurring income, refunds,

18
00:01:07,144 --> 00:01:11,603
currency conversions, fees and a few ambiguous movements.

19
00:01:11,603 --> 00:01:17,861
The tool classifies them, converts the amounts to euros, and flags the ambiguous

20
00:01:17,861 --> 00:01:20,052
movements for manual review.

21
00:01:20,052 --> 00:01:26,232
It also writes the CSV, the natural-language summary, and a visual HTML report.

22
00:01:26,233 --> 00:01:31,521
The whole flow is covered by thirty-one tests that run without network or

23
00:01:31,521 --> 00:01:32,390
credentials.

24
00:01:32,390 --> 00:01:38,112
The repository contains the PayPal client, the classifier, the pipeline and the

25
00:01:38,112 --> 00:01:39,126
documentation.

26
00:01:39,126 --> 00:01:44,848
Every real component has a safe fallback, so a missing key or a network failure

27
00:01:44,848 --> 00:01:46,441
cannot break the demo.

28
00:01:46,441 --> 00:01:50,336
The same command works against the PayPal sandbox.

29
00:01:50,336 --> 00:01:56,490
Here it is with real sandbox credentials: OAuth authentication, the Transaction

30
00:01:56,490 --> 00:02:00,151
Search API, and the same classification output.

31
00:02:00,151 --> 00:02:01,708
No real money moves.

32
00:02:01,708 --> 00:02:07,706
The sandbox lets you test the integration end to end before connecting a live

33
00:02:07,706 --> 00:02:08,329
account.

34
00:02:08,341 --> 00:02:13,351
The classifier can also run on a local large language model through an

35
00:02:13,351 --> 00:02:18,360
OpenAI-compatible endpoint, so the demo does not depend on a paid API.

36
00:02:18,360 --> 00:02:24,371
If the model is unavailable or returns an invalid answer, the tool falls back to the

37
00:02:24,371 --> 00:02:27,877
heuristic classifier, transaction by transaction.

38
00:02:27,877 --> 00:02:33,243
It is useful for freelancers, occasional sellers and anyone who receives money

39
00:02:33,243 --> 00:02:38,678
through PayPal and wants a clear starting point before filing their Spanish tax

40
00:02:38,678 --> 00:02:39,159
return.

41
00:02:39,159 --> 00:02:44,869
It is not tax advice, but it turns a messy statement into a reviewable first draft.

42
00:02:44,877 --> 00:02:48,619
The code is open source under the MIT license.

43
00:02:48,619 --> 00:02:50,571
Link in the description.

44
00:02:50,571 --> 00:02:55,125
Thanks for watching, and good luck with your tax return.
